# KNOWN GAP (DOCS) — `docs/language-design.md` §10's OPENING concurrency example
# does not typecheck. Two independent defects in five lines of doc.
#
#     async void bump(Shared[Mutex[int]] ^counter):
#         auto guard = counter.lock().unwrap()
#         *guard += 1
#
#     gg check -> rc 1, E_NoMethodFound:
#       no method `lock` found on type `Shared[Mutex[int]]`
#
# DEFECT 1 — `counter.lock()`. The COMPILER IS RIGHT and the DOC IS WRONG.
# D36 Q1 (ratified 2026-07-27) puts `Shared`/`Mutex`/`RWLock`/`Weak` in
# NonDerefContainer and rejects auto-deref for EVERY face: "users write
# `.lock()` / `.read()` / `.write()` / `.upgrade()` explicitly" -- i.e. on the
# Mutex, not through the Shared. §9.3 of the same document states this
# correctly eight sections earlier, so §10 contradicts §9.3.
#
# DEFECT 2 — `*guard += 1`. `*` accepts `Box[T]` ONLY; anything else is
# `E_DerefNonBox` (hardened deliberately in `47ab5a8e`, 2026-06-17, and
# re-affirmed by D48 2026-08-27). A `Guard` is not a `Box`. This line is
# unreachable behind defect 1 today, so fixing only defect 1 exposes it.
#
# NOT the `*box` question: D48 sanctions `*` on `Box[T]`. Nothing in D48
# widens the deref-able set, and this example is not an argument for widening
# it -- the guard's write face is `guard.method()` per D36 Q1.
#
# This is the "code suggested by our own documentation" class: a reader
# copying the doc's first concurrency example gets a check error, and the
# shape they would reach for next is also rejected.
#
# INTENDED: the DOC is corrected to the D36-ratified spelling; this fixture
# then pins the corrected shape. It is NOT a request to widen `*` or to make
# `Shared` auto-deref -- both were ratified the other way.
#
# Measured 2026-08-27 at HEAD in the doc's EXACT spelling; orchestrator-verified.

from std.sync import Mutex

async void bump(Shared[Mutex[int]] ^counter):
    auto guard = counter.lock().unwrap()
    *guard += 1

void main():
    print(1)
